# .mailmap — one person, one identity
#
# Git records whatever name and email were configured on the machine that made the
# commit. Anyone who has ever committed from two laptops, changed jobs, or fixed a
# typo in `git config user.name` therefore shows up as several different people in
# `git shortlog`, `git blame`, and the repository's Contributors graph.
#
# This file collapses those back into one person. It changes what git *reports*; it
# does not rewrite a single commit.
#
# Format — canonical identity first, then the variant to fold into it:
#
#   Preferred Name <preferred@email>  <old-or-wrong@email>
#   Preferred Name <preferred@email>  Old Name <old@email>
#
# The first form maps by email alone (the common case: same person, second address).
# The second also replaces a wrong name. Comments and blank lines are ignored.
#
# Verify your entry works:
#
#   git shortlog -sne          # one line per person, not one per address
#   git check-mailmap "Name <email>"
#
# Adding yourself here is a correction to the record and never needs justification.
# Do not add an entry that maps one person's commits onto a different person.

# --- entries below this line ---
